    "summary": "Smoky Cryptical‑Uncle suite, monster China>Rider, tight jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a blazing Cryptical Envelopment → Uncle → The Other One trek and a monster China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider. Jerry's Telecaster tone shines, Phil erupts on bass, and Pigpen adds soulful organ before his brief Chicago hiatus.",
    "review": "On August 23, 1971 at the Auditorium Theatre in Chicago, the Dead rolled out two marathon sets that still feel fresh. The first set builds solidly through classics like \"Me And Bobby McGee\" and a bright \"China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider\" before diving into an unforgettable second‑set suite: a smoking \"Cryptical Envelopment → Me And My Uncle → Cryptical Envelopment\" morphs seamlessly into a high‑octane \"The Other One\" marathon that rockets through \"Wharf Rat\" and lands on a soaring \"Bird Song.\" Jerry’s Telecaster is praised for its crisp tone, Phil “pops off” with mad eighth‑note bass runs, Bob provides rock‑steady rhythm and vocal support, and Pigpen’s organ/vocals add a poignant touch as this marks his last Chicago run before a brief hiatus. Despite a few patched sections in the source tape, the soundboard transfer captures the band’s energy brilliantly, making this a must‑listen snapshot of the Summer ’71 era.\n\nWhile an audience recording of the show exists, its muffled quality and missing tracks render it nearly unlistenable; the Miller soundboard release stands out as the definitive listening experience with clear, crisp mix and only minor patchwork. Fans seeking the full arc—from \"Big Railroad Blues\" through the final jubilant \"Johnny B. Goode\"—should start with the SBD for its fidelity and completeness.",
